1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,072
You've got a hundred pounds spare each month.

2
00:00:03,072 --> 00:00:05,352
Put it on the mortgage, or invest it?

3
00:00:05,352 --> 00:00:07,878
Take the mortgage from our first episode.

4
00:00:07,878 --> 00:00:11,389
Over twenty-five years, the gap between those two choices

5
00:00:11,389 --> 00:00:14,593
can run to thousands of pounds, in either direction.

6
00:00:14,593 --> 00:00:18,351
And one number decides which way it tips: your mortgage rate.

7
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:25,564
In Episode 03, we saw what overpaying does: the same

8
00:00:25,564 --> 00:00:29,218
mortgage, gone three and a half years early, with twenty-four

9
00:00:29,218 --> 00:00:32,392
and a half thousand pounds of interest you never pay.

10
00:00:32,392 --> 00:00:35,027
Today's question is whether you should do it

11
00:00:35,027 --> 00:00:37,183
at all, or invest the money instead.

12
00:00:37,183 --> 00:00:39,339
We'll put the two side by side, find

13
00:00:39,339 --> 00:00:41,795
the number that decides it, and then look

14
00:00:41,795 --> 00:00:44,190
at the things to sort out before either.

15
00:00:44,190 --> 00:00:46,825
This is general information, not advice, and

16
00:00:46,825 --> 00:00:49,461
there's no single right answer for everyone.

17
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:52,602
Here's the mortgage again.

18
00:00:52,602 --> 00:00:57,403
Two hundred thousand pounds, over twenty-five years, at five per cent fixed.

19
00:00:57,403 --> 00:01:01,952
One thousand, one hundred and sixty-nine pounds, eighteen pence a month.

20
00:01:01,952 --> 00:01:04,542
Every pound you overpay stops costing you

21
00:01:04,542 --> 00:01:06,626
five per cent a year in interest.

22
00:01:06,626 --> 00:01:08,837
So overpaying earns you a return of

23
00:01:08,837 --> 00:01:11,490
five per cent, exactly your mortgage rate.

24
00:01:11,490 --> 00:01:13,764
Think of it as a guaranteed five per

25
00:01:13,764 --> 00:01:16,038
cent, from a bank that can't say no.

26
00:01:16,038 --> 00:01:18,692
No market can take it away, and there's no

27
00:01:18,692 --> 00:01:21,471
tax to pay on interest you simply never pay.

28
00:01:22,970 --> 00:01:24,532
Investing is different.

29
00:01:24,532 --> 00:01:27,724
Over long periods, a broad spread of shares has

30
00:01:27,724 --> 00:01:30,781
often grown faster than five per cent a year.

31
00:01:30,781 --> 00:01:34,177
But that's an expected return, not a promised one.

32
00:01:34,177 --> 00:01:35,603
Some years, it falls.

33
00:01:35,603 --> 00:01:37,301
Picture a pair of scales.

34
00:01:37,301 --> 00:01:41,172
On one side, a solid block: the guaranteed five per cent.

35
00:01:41,172 --> 00:01:45,383
On the other, a block that wobbles: what investing might earn.

36
00:01:45,383 --> 00:01:47,828
Much of the time, it may weigh more.

37
00:01:47,828 --> 00:01:50,137
You just can't be sure in advance.

38
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:54,358
And before you compare, take off the costs.

39
00:01:54,358 --> 00:01:57,076
Fund and platform fees come out every year.

40
00:01:57,076 --> 00:01:59,604
Tax can take a slice too, unless you use

41
00:01:59,604 --> 00:02:02,954
the tax-free or tax-relieved accounts where you live.

42
00:02:02,954 --> 00:02:05,672
A fund that grows seven per cent, minus one

43
00:02:05,672 --> 00:02:08,201
per cent in fees, is really earning six.

44
00:02:08,201 --> 00:02:11,045
So the fair comparison is your mortgage rate,

45
00:02:11,045 --> 00:02:14,079
against what investing earns after fees and tax.

46
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:18,349
Let's put them head to head, with one hundred

47
00:02:18,349 --> 00:02:21,303
pounds a month, over the full twenty-five years.

48
00:02:21,303 --> 00:02:22,411
Option A overpays.

49
00:02:22,411 --> 00:02:26,227
The mortgage is gone after two hundred and fifty-eight months.

50
00:02:26,227 --> 00:02:29,489
Then the whole payment it frees up, one thousand, two

51
00:02:29,489 --> 00:02:33,427
hundred and sixty-nine pounds, eighteen pence a month, goes into

52
00:02:33,427 --> 00:02:36,381
investments for the last three and a half years.

53
00:02:36,381 --> 00:02:39,335
Option B pays the mortgage normally, and invests

54
00:02:39,335 --> 00:02:42,043
the hundred pounds every month from day one.

55
00:02:42,043 --> 00:02:45,613
Either way, after twenty-five years, the mortgage is gone.

56
00:02:45,613 --> 00:02:48,259
The question is which leaves you with more.

57
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:53,025
Here's the result, if investing earns five per cent

58
00:02:53,025 --> 00:02:55,714
after fees, the same as the mortgage rate.

59
00:02:55,714 --> 00:03:01,028
Option A ends with fifty-nine thousand, five hundred and fifty-one pounds invested.

60
00:03:01,028 --> 00:03:06,022
Option B ends with fifty-nine thousand, five hundred and fifty-one pounds too.

61
00:03:06,022 --> 00:03:06,918
Exactly level.

62
00:03:06,918 --> 00:03:08,519
Think of it as a see-saw.

63
00:03:08,519 --> 00:03:13,512
When the investment return matches your mortgage rate, it sits perfectly flat.

64
00:03:13,512 --> 00:03:16,777
Nudge the return up, and it tips towards investing.

65
00:03:16,777 --> 00:03:19,722
Nudge it down, and it tips towards overpaying.

66
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:22,458
Why exactly level?

67
00:03:22,458 --> 00:03:25,003
Because at five per cent, every pound

68
00:03:25,003 --> 00:03:26,998
does the same job either way.

69
00:03:26,998 --> 00:03:31,125
A pound overpaid saves five per cent a year on the mortgage.

70
00:03:31,125 --> 00:03:34,908
A pound invested earns five per cent a year in the pot.

71
00:03:34,908 --> 00:03:37,316
Same rate, same pound, same result.

72
00:03:37,316 --> 00:03:41,099
That's why the return after fees is the whole question.

73
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:44,972
Now let's try three different returns.

74
00:03:44,972 --> 00:03:47,781
At four per cent after fees, overpaying comes

75
00:03:47,781 --> 00:03:50,965
out ahead, by seven thousand and eighty-two pounds.

76
00:03:50,965 --> 00:03:53,399
At six per cent, investing is ahead, by

77
00:03:53,399 --> 00:03:56,583
eight thousand, six hundred and sixty-eight pounds.

78
00:03:56,583 --> 00:03:59,142
And at seven per cent, investing is ahead

79
00:03:59,142 --> 00:04:02,451
by nineteen thousand, two hundred and seventy pounds.

80
00:04:02,451 --> 00:04:05,697
So the deciding number really is your mortgage rate.

81
00:04:05,697 --> 00:04:07,756
Expect to earn more than it after

82
00:04:07,756 --> 00:04:09,816
fees, and investing has the edge.

83
00:04:09,816 --> 00:04:11,315
If not, overpaying does.

84
00:04:11,315 --> 00:04:14,186
And if you'd choose a cautious fund that holds

85
00:04:14,186 --> 00:04:17,182
bonds as well as shares, compare a lower number,

86
00:04:17,182 --> 00:04:19,929
because those usually aim for lower returns.

87
00:04:21,430 --> 00:04:24,130
Here's the whole comparison in one table.

88
00:04:24,130 --> 00:04:27,092
At four per cent after fees, overpaying wins.

89
00:04:27,092 --> 00:04:28,409
At five, it's a tie.

90
00:04:28,409 --> 00:04:31,306
At six and seven, investing pulls ahead, and

91
00:04:31,306 --> 00:04:34,072
the gap widens the higher the return goes.

92
00:04:34,072 --> 00:04:36,640
But look at how much each column moves.

93
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:39,603
The overpay column changes by just over three

94
00:04:39,603 --> 00:04:42,763
thousand pounds from top to bottom, because most

95
00:04:42,763 --> 00:04:44,409
of its gain is locked in.

96
00:04:44,409 --> 00:04:47,965
The investing column swings by almost thirty thousand.

97
00:04:47,965 --> 00:04:50,269
That swing is the risk you take on.

98
00:04:51,770 --> 00:04:54,464
Before we go on, a question for you.

99
00:04:54,464 --> 00:04:57,532
Overpay, invest, or split the difference?

100
00:04:57,532 --> 00:04:59,927
Which would you choose, and why?

101
00:04:59,927 --> 00:05:01,723
Tell us in the comments.

102
00:05:03,220 --> 00:05:05,125
One thing the averages hide.

103
00:05:05,125 --> 00:05:07,914
Investment returns don't arrive smoothly.

104
00:05:07,914 --> 00:05:10,634
In the two thousands, global shares went

105
00:05:10,634 --> 00:05:12,947
almost nowhere for a whole decade.

106
00:05:12,947 --> 00:05:16,144
A bad decade, especially near the end, when the

107
00:05:16,144 --> 00:05:18,933
pot is biggest, can pull a seven per cent

108
00:05:18,933 --> 00:05:21,042
plan below a five per cent one.

109
00:05:21,042 --> 00:05:23,423
Overpaying can't have a bad decade.

110
00:05:23,423 --> 00:05:27,096
That's what the guaranteed return buys you: certainty.

111
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:31,303
And remember, your mortgage rate can change.

112
00:05:31,303 --> 00:05:33,514
When a fixed deal ends, the rate you

113
00:05:33,514 --> 00:05:35,970
move to becomes the new deciding number.

114
00:05:35,970 --> 00:05:38,366
When mortgage rates were around two per

115
00:05:38,366 --> 00:05:41,191
cent, investing usually looked the better bet.

116
00:05:41,191 --> 00:05:43,771
At five per cent, it's a much closer call.

117
00:05:43,771 --> 00:05:48,009
So it's worth running the comparison again every time you remortgage.

118
00:05:49,510 --> 00:05:53,721
Whichever you lean towards, there are three things to sort first.

119
00:05:53,721 --> 00:05:57,608
Think of them as rungs on a ladder, climbed from the bottom.

120
00:05:57,608 --> 00:06:01,107
First, an emergency fund: money you can reach quickly.

121
00:06:01,107 --> 00:06:03,958
Overpaid money is locked into the house, and

122
00:06:03,958 --> 00:06:06,873
unless your mortgage lets you borrow it back,

123
00:06:06,873 --> 00:06:09,076
you can't easily get it out again.

124
00:06:09,076 --> 00:06:14,388
Second, if your employer matches pension contributions, take the full match first.

125
00:06:14,388 --> 00:06:17,952
That's often an instant return far above five per cent.

126
00:06:17,952 --> 00:06:20,673
Third, clear any debts that cost more than

127
00:06:20,673 --> 00:06:23,783
your mortgage, like credit cards or car finance.

128
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:27,792
That pension match deserves a closer look.

129
00:06:27,792 --> 00:06:29,944
Say you pay in fifty pounds a month,

130
00:06:29,944 --> 00:06:32,815
and your employer matches it with another fifty.

131
00:06:32,815 --> 00:06:35,625
That's a hundred pounds going into your pension

132
00:06:35,625 --> 00:06:38,077
for fifty of your own: double your money,

133
00:06:38,077 --> 00:06:40,230
before any investment growth at all.

134
00:06:40,230 --> 00:06:42,981
No mortgage overpayment can compete with that.

135
00:06:42,981 --> 00:06:47,406
Check your scheme's rules, because matches vary from employer to employer.

136
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:50,762
Then check the mortgage itself.

137
00:06:50,762 --> 00:06:53,705
Many fixed-rate deals let you overpay up to about

138
00:06:53,705 --> 00:06:55,987
ten per cent of the balance each year.

139
00:06:55,987 --> 00:06:57,909
Go over your limit, and an early

140
00:06:57,909 --> 00:07:00,252
repayment charge can wipe out the gain.

141
00:07:00,252 --> 00:07:03,014
Your lender can tell you your exact allowance.

142
00:07:03,014 --> 00:07:04,876
And think about how you'd feel.

143
00:07:04,876 --> 00:07:07,099
If a market fall would keep you awake

144
00:07:07,099 --> 00:07:09,922
at night, the guaranteed return has real value,

145
00:07:09,922 --> 00:07:12,384
even when the spreadsheet says otherwise.

146
00:07:13,890 --> 00:07:16,356
And it doesn't have to be all or nothing.

147
00:07:16,356 --> 00:07:18,040
Many people split the money.

148
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:19,062
Picture two jars.

149
00:07:19,062 --> 00:07:20,986
Fifty pounds a month goes on the

150
00:07:20,986 --> 00:07:23,632
mortgage, and fifty pounds into investments.

151
00:07:23,632 --> 00:07:26,880
You get some of the certainty, and some of the growth.

152
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:30,669
In our example, the mortgage still ends nearly two years early.

153
00:07:30,669 --> 00:07:32,894
And at six per cent after fees, you'd

154
00:07:32,894 --> 00:07:36,442
finish with about sixty-four thousand, seven hundred pounds

155
00:07:36,442 --> 00:07:38,968
invested, roughly halfway between the two.

156
00:07:40,470 --> 00:07:42,689
So, three things to take away.

157
00:07:42,689 --> 00:07:46,609
One: overpaying earns your mortgage rate, guaranteed.

158
00:07:46,609 --> 00:07:50,012
Two: investing only wins if it beats that rate

159
00:07:50,012 --> 00:07:53,414
after fees and tax, and that isn't guaranteed.

160
00:07:53,414 --> 00:07:57,334
Three: whichever you choose, sort the emergency fund,

161
00:07:57,334 --> 00:08:00,663
the pension match and any dearer debts first.

162
00:08:02,160 --> 00:08:03,857
So here's the one number.

163
00:08:03,857 --> 00:08:06,707
Write down your mortgage rate, and next to

164
00:08:06,707 --> 00:08:09,557
it, a realistic return after fees and tax.

165
00:08:09,557 --> 00:08:12,476
If the return is clearly higher, and you've

166
00:08:12,476 --> 00:08:15,665
sorted the three rungs, investing has the edge.

167
00:08:15,665 --> 00:08:20,077
If it's close, or lower, or you value certainty, overpaying wins.

168
00:08:20,077 --> 00:08:24,013
This is general information, not advice, so check your own

169
00:08:24,013 --> 00:08:27,814
deal, and speak to a regulated adviser if you're unsure.

170
00:08:27,814 --> 00:08:31,207
Next Thursday: that one per cent fee we mentioned.

171
00:08:31,207 --> 00:08:32,225
It sounds tiny.

172
00:08:32,225 --> 00:08:34,261
Over a working life, it isn't.

173
00:08:34,261 --> 00:08:36,433
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
